Fixed — [bizdays.py](bizdays.py) now does an O(1) weekday count instead of a day-by-day loop, and all 11 tests pass.

**What changed:**
- `_weekday_count(d0, d1)` computes Mon–Fri days in an inclusive range with pure arithmetic: `weeks*5` plus a check of the ≤6 leftover days. No loop over the range, so a 100-year span is instant.
- `business_days` now:
  - includes **both** endpoints (the old `while d < end` skipped `end`);
  - accepts datetimes (`.date()` extracted for start, end and holidays);
  - deduplicates holidays via a set, ignores weekend holidays and ones outside the range;
  - swaps start/end and negates when `end < start`.

**Tests** ([test_bizdays.py](test_bizdays.py)): inclusive ends, weekend-only ranges, holidays (in-range, weekend, duplicate, out-of-range), reversed/negative, datetimes, a 100-year case (26090, brute-force verified), and a 200-case randomized differential check against a naive brute-force implementation. One note: my first hardcoded constant for the 100-year test was wrong (a guess); I computed the real value by brute force and corrected the test — the implementation itself was right.
